WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:38.261
Nietzsche talking about the European state
of mind at the end of the 19th century, so

00:00:38.261 --> 00:00:41.329
Nietzsche says: well, we’re in this terrible
situation, right?

00:00:41.329 --> 00:00:44.530
God is dead, we’ve killed him, what does
that mean?

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:50.140
Well, we’ve taken our evolved metaphysics
which structures our moral viewpoint and undermined

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:54.870
it by rational criticism – peculiar move
philosophically because it was never established

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:56.360
on rational grounds anyways.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:01.149
We’ve undermined it rationally and replaced
it with, well, nothing, nothing.

00:01:01.149 --> 00:01:03.420
What’s the consequence of that?

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.710
Well, he outlines that here.

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:11.350
“Of what is great, one must either be silent
or speak with greatness.”

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:16.000
And you can think about this as a prophesy
on the events of World War I and World War

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:22.569
II and the Gulag archipelago and the 60 million
people dead in the Soviet Union and the whole

00:01:22.569 --> 00:01:28.110
unfolding of 20th century history and the
great ideological battles that characterized

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:29.110
that unfolding.

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:31.990
So this is something Nietzsche sees coming
and knows why.

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:34.690
He says:
“of what is great, one must either be silent

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:36.350
or speak with greatness.

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:38.189
With greatness.

00:01:38.189 --> 00:01:41.030
That means cynically and with innocence.

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:45.509
What I relate of the history of the next two
centuries, I describe what is coming, what

00:01:45.509 --> 00:01:47.740
can no longer come differently.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:51.579
The advent of nihilism, right, the belief
in nothing.

00:01:51.579 --> 00:01:55.799
Our whole European culture is moving for some
time now with a tortured tension that’s

00:01:55.799 --> 00:02:02.310
growing from decade to decade as towards a
catastrophe, restlessly, violently, headlong

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:05.950
like a river that wants to reach the end that
no longer reflects.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:08.590
It’s afraid to reflect.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:13.660
He that speaks here has conversely done nothing
so far but to reflect as a philosopher and

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:19.889
solitary by instinct who has found his advantage
in standing aside outside.

00:02:19.889 --> 00:02:22.440
“
Why does the advent of nihilism become necessary?

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:28.640
Well, because the values we’ve had hitherto
thus draw their final consequence because

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:33.900
nihilism represents the ultimate logical conclusion
of our great values and ideals because we

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:39.920
must experience nihilism before we can find
out what value these values really have.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:43.889
We require at some time new values.

00:02:43.889 --> 00:02:48.970
Nihilism stands at the door whence comes this
uncanniest of all guests.

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:52.199
Nihilism, right?

00:02:52.199 --> 00:02:56.150
Your beliefs are undermined once, what’s
the consequence of that?

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:59.350
Well, one consequence is the belief is undermined.

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:05.570
The other consequence is more metaphysical,
which is: fooled once, you no longer have

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:06.570
the belief.

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:09.199
But maybe it’s even worse than that because
human beings can generalize.

00:03:09.199 --> 00:03:13.610
Fooled once, you never have any, you no longer
have any belief in beliefs,

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:17.501
which means you say something like this: I
don’t care what you think, doesn’t matter

00:03:17.501 --> 00:03:22.540
what you think, the world is such a terrible
place that no interpretation whatsoever can

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:24.000
possibly suffice.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.510
That’s nihilism.

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:28.880
No meaning system whatsoever can possibly
suffice.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:30.769
Well, what’s the flaw?

00:03:30.769 --> 00:03:36.200
Well, the flaw is: well of course no system
of coherent belief can suffice because most

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:37.940
of the world’s transcendent.

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:42.259
You can’t encapsulate everything that is,
in your sphere of belief

00:03:42.259 --> 00:03:46.290
and what you might say then is that if you
ever believe that what you believe is what

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:51.350
should support you, the facts you know say,
or the interpretation you place on the world,

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:53.820
then your faith is badly misplaced.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:57.500
You don't believe in what you believe, you
believe in something that’s deeper than

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:01.100
that
And so then you see what’s wrong with Tolstoy,

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:11.100
right, and Tolstoy’s story and Tolstoy says,
accounting for his collapse in the stability

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:15.190
of Christian belief,
he said: this all happened, this collapse

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:18.009
of my belief, when I was not yet 50 years
old.

00:04:18.009 --> 00:04:20.389
I should have been considered a completely
happy man.

00:04:20.389 --> 00:04:25.720
I had a good, loving, beloved wife, fine children
and a large estate growing and expanding without

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:27.340
any effort on my part.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:32.070
I was respected by friends and acquaintances,
praised by strangers and could claim a certain

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:33.220
renown.

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:36.070
I was not physically nor mentally unhealthy.

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:39.920
On the contrary, I enjoyed a physical and
mental vigour I had rarely encountered among

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:41.130
others my age.

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:44.680
I could keep up with the peasants working
in the fields and work eight and ten hours

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:48.590
at a stretch without suffering any after-effects
from the strain.

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:53.120
And in such a state of affairs, I came to
a point where I could not live and even though

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:58.600
I feared death, I had to employ ruses against
myself to keep from committing suicide.

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:03.910
It was as though I had lived a little, wandered
a little until I came to a precipice and I

00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:06.870
clearly saw that there was nothing ahead except
ruin.

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:11.120
And there was no stopping or turning back,
no closing my eyes so that I would not see

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:15.290
that there was nothing ahead except the deception
of life and of happiness and of the reality

00:05:15.290 --> 00:05:18.310
of suffering and death, of complete annihilation.

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:20.600
I grew sick of life.

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:24.370
Some irresistible force was leading me to
somehow get rid of it.

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:28.840
this thought was such a temptation that I
had to use cunning against myself in order

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:30.410
not to go through with it.

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:35.610
And there I was, a fortunate man carrying
a rope from my room where I was alone every

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:39.930
night as I undressed so that I would not hang
myself from the beam between the closets.

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:43.790
And I quit going hunting with a gun so that
I would not be too easily tempted to rid myself

00:05:43.790 --> 00:05:44.790
of life.

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:48.660
I myself did not know what I wanted, was afraid
of life.

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:52.250
I struggled to get rid of it yet I hoped for
something from it.

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:53.710
My position was terrible.

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:58.410
I knew that I could find nothing in the way
of rational knowledge except a denial of life

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:01.900
and in faith I could find nothing except a
denial of reason.

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:06.830
And for me this was even more impossible than
a denial of life.

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:11.090
according to rational knowledge, it followed
that life is evil and people know it.

00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:17.590
I described my spiritual condition to myself
in this way: my life is some kind of stupid

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:21.090
and evil practical joke that someone is playing
on me.

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:24.360
In spite of the fact that I did not acknowledge
the existence of any someone who might have

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:29.600
created me, the notion that someone brought
me into the world as a stupid and evil joke

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:34.090
seemed to be the most natural way to describe
my condition.

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:35.890
I could not be deceived.

00:06:35.890 --> 00:06:41.420
All is vanity, happy is he who’s never been
born, death is better than life, we must rid

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:43.520
ourselves of life.

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:47.030
Having realized all the stupidity of the joke
that is being played on us, and seeing that

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:50.310
the blessings of the dead are greater than
those of the living and that it’s better

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:55.090
not to exist, the strong act and put an end
to this stupid joke and they use any means

00:06:55.090 --> 00:07:01.710
of doing it – a rope around the neck, water,
a knife in the heart or a train.

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:05.620
Now the interesting thing about this, I think,
is first of all, a Russian wrote it; and second

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:07.900
of all, it was written in the late 1900s.

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:12.150
And even more particularly is that you note
that when the strong act, using a rope around

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:17.270
the neck, water, a knife in the heart and
a train, well, Tolstoy’s talking about suicide.

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:21.560
There’s no necessary reason to presume that
this should only be violence engendered against

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:22.640
the self, right?

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:29.190
If life is a stupid and evil joke, then what’s
stopping you from benevolently putting an

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:32.520
end to the suffering of others, right?

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:37.560
Benevolently in theory at least.

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:43.460
Well, you know that’s one perspective, right,
but then there’s always the perspective

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:47.310
of the lady who went to see the psychiatrist
in T.S.

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:52.710
Eliot’s poem, which is: well, if when your
eyes are open life appears as nothing but

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:57.230
suffering and pain to you, it could be that
that is how life is.

00:07:57.230 --> 00:08:00.550
But it could also be that there’s something
wrong with the way that you’re looking at

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:01.550
the world.

00:08:01.550 --> 00:08:05.400
And in some ways, that’s a much more humble
perspective, right, because the alternative

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:09.120
is: well, I know what’s going on and I just
look out there and there’s the world and

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.370
I’ve pretty much got it, like I know what
it means and what it means is pointless suffering

00:08:13.370 --> 00:08:17.479
and pain and that’s my model and I don't
see any reason to question it.

00:08:17.479 --> 00:08:21.870
But then the alternative is: well, wait a
second, there’s always the possibility that

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:26.810
I don’t know absolutely everything and this
final and horrible judgment that I’m placing

00:08:26.810 --> 00:08:33.780
on the conditions of existence could conceivably
be misplaced, given the sort of presumptuousness

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:34.990
of the claim,
right?

00:08:34.990 --> 00:08:41.820
I’m in a position to render final judgment
on the moral value of existence as such.

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:48.240
It seems to me reasonable to presume that
that’s not the kind of statement that you

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:53.680
should easily make
And I remember when George Bush launched his

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:59.140
most recent war, the initial terminology,
I think this was for the Afghanistan battle,

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:01.710
was: Operation Infinite Justice.

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:07.899
But he retracted that phrase after a number
of religious leaders objected to its kind

00:09:07.899 --> 00:09:10.930
of presumptuousness –
which I thought was quite reasonable because

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:16.481
infinite justice is something that most people
should probably not hope for, right, because

00:09:16.481 --> 00:09:18.779
you never know precisely what infinite justice
means

00:09:18.779 --> 00:09:23.579
because it might just mean that every bloody
mistake you’ve ever made, you’re going

00:09:23.579 --> 00:09:24.600
to pay for.

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:30.420
And I suppose that would be just as applicable
to George W. Bush as it would to anybody else

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:35.510
And then Milton again describes the development
of this adversarial spirit.

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:39.319
He says: first, pride, pride and worse, ambition,
threw me down.

00:09:39.319 --> 00:09:41.910
That’s Satan’s lament when he’s in hell.

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:44.499
And Milton’s description of hell is extremely
interesting.

00:09:44.499 --> 00:09:48.980
He said: the reason that hell is characterized
by its structure is not so much because of

00:09:48.980 --> 00:09:53.529
its nature precisely, it’s because of its
distance from the good.

00:09:53.529 --> 00:09:59.600
So the farther you are away, say, from what
constitutes the good, the more suffering is

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:01.249
endemic to that state.

00:10:01.249 --> 00:10:04.569
So it’s the distance away from something
that constitutes the suffering.

00:10:04.569 --> 00:10:09.069
And then Milton says: it’s very interesting
to do an analysis of Satan’s character and

00:10:09.069 --> 00:10:13.889
the notion of hell per se because how in the
world can you reconcile the idea of a good

00:10:13.889 --> 00:10:16.649
God with the notion of this continual suffering?

00:10:16.649 --> 00:10:21.250
Milton says: well, Satan can step out of hell
in one moment, all he has to do is admit that

00:10:21.250 --> 00:10:22.360
he was wrong.

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:28.769
And that’s the one thing that he will not
do under any circumstances whatsoever

00:10:28.769 --> 00:10:31.730
So then we put one more twist on the story
and we say something like this:

00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:36.250
OK, we already know that part of the reason
that people have belief systems is so that

00:10:36.250 --> 00:10:38.620
they can structure their interactions with
the world.

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:41.059
It’s a tool box, say.

00:10:41.059 --> 00:10:44.970
We’re playing a game, we share the rules,
that’s fine, we can cooperate with one another.

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:49.339
It could be other than it is but it’s the
way it is and it works for us, that’s fine.

00:10:49.339 --> 00:10:53.369
There’s nothing absolute about it except
that a structure like that is necessary.

00:10:53.369 --> 00:10:58.959
Now whenever there’s a threat to that shared
view of the world, well then we’re afraid

00:10:58.959 --> 00:11:03.499
and for good reason and it’s not surprising
under those circumstances that we fight to

00:11:03.499 --> 00:11:06.199
defend what we’ve made ours.

00:11:06.199 --> 00:11:12.769
But then you say, say you adopt this perspective,
right, and it’s this vengeful desire to

00:11:12.769 --> 00:11:18.820
destroy that extends beyond other individuals
and beyond society even to the structure of

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:20.019
experience as such.

00:11:20.019 --> 00:11:24.240
And then you think: well, what’s the best
mask for that and how do these two processes

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:25.550
sort of interact?

00:11:25.550 --> 00:11:33.379
And you think: well, the most efficient way
to do terrible things is to mask them with

00:11:33.379 --> 00:11:37.529
the highest order of morality and that’s
precisely what the totalitarian does.

00:11:37.529 --> 00:11:39.639
So that way, he gets to have his cake and
eat it too.

00:11:39.639 --> 00:11:44.679
He’s perfectly well protected from apprehension
of the world because his belief system is

00:11:44.679 --> 00:11:50.160
complete, plus his underground motivations,
which is this constant desire for revenge,

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:55.689
can find their expression within the totalitarian
structure and remain invisible even to himself.

00:11:55.689 --> 00:12:01.660
So he can say to himself: well, the reason
I threw all those farmers out of their house

00:12:01.660 --> 00:12:07.339
in 1920 and stole their soup and their food
and their grandmother’s blankets and everything

00:12:07.339 --> 00:12:12.029
they’d worked to own was because I was building
a socialist paradise, right?

00:12:12.029 --> 00:12:15.610
And it was a good thing for me to go into
that house and not a bad thing.

00:12:15.610 --> 00:12:19.580
And as long as he believes that, or acts as
if he believes that, then he can look in the

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:24.310
mirror without screaming and there’s no
recognition whatsoever of precisely the sort

00:12:24.310 --> 00:12:28.149
of game that he’s involved in, so he has
it both ways, right?

00:12:28.149 --> 00:12:32.720
He can do everything terrible that he always
dreams of doing and consider himself not only

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:39.449
good but good even at a higher level than
the people that he was actually afflicting.

00:12:39.449 --> 00:12:47.950
And of course, that’s just standard description
of what happened in the Soviet Union.

00:12:47.950 --> 00:12:53.360
Nietzsche says, I love this, his definition
of morality, it’s the most cynical thing

00:12:53.360 --> 00:13:00.749
Nietzsche ever said, I think: “the idiosyncrasy
of decadence with the ulterior motive of revenging

00:13:00.749 --> 00:13:03.329
oneself against life successfully.

00:13:03.329 --> 00:13:06.339
I attach value to this definition.”

00:13:06.339 --> 00:13:08.519
He said: well, why be an ideologue?

00:13:08.519 --> 00:13:11.079
Well, it’s a good way to simplify the world,
right?

00:13:11.079 --> 00:13:15.170
It’s a procrustean bed, you just chop off
everything that doesn’t fit, then you don’t

00:13:15.170 --> 00:13:16.170
have to think, right?

00:13:16.170 --> 00:13:19.999
So that’s good because thinking is difficult
and it’s troublesome and it takes courage

00:13:19.999 --> 00:13:23.920
and so forth to transform chaos into order
is no trivial matter.

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:27.829
And if it’s all ordered for you, well then
there’s really nothing left for you to do.

00:13:27.829 --> 00:13:29.709
But then Nietzsche goes even below that.

00:13:29.709 --> 00:13:31.809
He says: yeah, well there’s more to the
story than that, isn’t there?

00:13:31.809 --> 00:13:37.459
It’s like once you got this little procrustean
bed all arranged for your enemies, then you

00:13:37.459 --> 00:13:44.559
can allow your most base, vengeful instincts
full flow by just continually chopping people

00:13:44.559 --> 00:13:46.050
so they fit.

00:13:46.050 --> 00:13:52.860
And you do it, all the while saying: well,
it’s obviously the best thing that could

00:13:52.860 --> 00:13:59.489
possibly be done.

00:13:59.489 --> 00:14:06.850
And so then you look at Stalin, say, because
not everybody who’s adopted a vengeful tack

00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:11.959
on existence is sort of like the archetype
of vengefulness or adversarial spirit.

00:14:11.959 --> 00:14:16.279
But you get now and then the people like Stalin,
who are good counter-examples, say, to the

00:14:16.279 --> 00:14:17.369
people like Gandhi.

00:14:17.369 --> 00:14:21.290
And so Stalin’s very instructive and so
we could start by looking at what he did in

00:14:21.290 --> 00:14:22.589
the Ukraine.

00:14:22.589 --> 00:14:27.850
So at the end of 1929, the Kremlin decreed
that millions of peasants from individually

00:14:27.850 --> 00:14:32.600
owned farms would be forced into agricultural
collectives or kolkhozes seen in the eyes

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:37.560
of the politburo as pliant providers of Soviet
agricultural needs.

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:42.720
In defiance of the facts, Soviet ideologists
hammered out an appropriate Marxist terminology

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:44.579
to explain what was going on.

00:14:44.579 --> 00:14:51.000
Throughout grain-producing areas, it was said:
resistance to this scientific scheme was being

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:54.119
organized by so-called rich peasants or Gulaks.

00:14:54.119 --> 00:14:59.270
With his customary brutality, therefore, Stalin
decreed the liquidation of the kulaks as a

00:14:59.270 --> 00:15:02.559
class
Well, Stalin liked this idea of like group

00:15:02.559 --> 00:15:04.839
guilt, that was a major theme for Stalin.

00:15:04.839 --> 00:15:08.069
That meant I really didn’t have to pay attention
to you as an individual.

00:15:08.069 --> 00:15:13.860
I could just decide if you were a doctor or
engineer or a kulak or a German or whatever

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:18.869
ethnic, racial or educational division happened
to characterize a particular target at the

00:15:18.869 --> 00:15:19.869
time.

00:15:19.869 --> 00:15:22.420
And it didn’t matter if you were guilty
as an individual.

00:15:22.420 --> 00:15:23.959
That whole notion never even obtained.

00:15:23.959 --> 00:15:27.720
It was class guilt that mattered and if you
were in one of those classes, well we’re

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:28.720
better without you.

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:30.939
And of course, the nature of the class just
changed constantly.

00:15:30.939 --> 00:15:36.100
But it was a perfectly logical thing to think
if you believed in like historical determinism.

00:15:36.100 --> 00:15:39.690
If your parents were rich bourgeois, what
was the probability that you’re going to

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:42.009
be a useful part of the workers’ collective?

00:15:42.009 --> 00:15:45.800
It would be easier just to get rid of you
ahead of time so you didn’t cause too much

00:15:45.800 --> 00:15:46.959
trouble.

00:15:46.959 --> 00:15:50.860
So then you think about these kulaks, rich
peasants, well who were these people?

00:15:50.860 --> 00:15:52.939
Like when we get down to the individual level?

00:15:52.939 --> 00:15:59.309
So you go in a village, the village was full
of serfs, like not 40 years before, so these

00:15:59.309 --> 00:16:01.810
are people just struggling out of the feudal
society,

00:16:01.810 --> 00:16:02.810
right?

00:16:02.810 --> 00:16:06.180
And you got some people in there who’ve
managed to be successful enough as farmers,

00:16:06.180 --> 00:16:10.149
which is no easy thing, to like have a house
and maybe hire one person.

00:16:10.149 --> 00:16:16.200
And you know maybe have a little extra food
in the larder and a few kind of material possessions.

00:16:16.200 --> 00:16:19.749
So these are successful people and so you
could say: well, they’re the ones that actually

00:16:19.749 --> 00:16:20.889
knew how to farm.

00:16:20.889 --> 00:16:21.889
That’s one theory.

00:16:21.889 --> 00:16:24.920
Or you could say: the reason they had all
this stuff was because they stole it from

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:25.999
all the other people, right?

00:16:25.999 --> 00:16:31.939
And then you think: OK, so I march into town,
I’m a Soviet revolutionary and I say: hey,

00:16:31.939 --> 00:16:35.939
guys, you know those rich people, they stole
everything they have from you.

00:16:35.939 --> 00:16:39.009
And then you think: OK, which of you guys
is going to listen to that?

00:16:39.009 --> 00:16:43.059
Well, it’s not going to be the sort of struggling
people just underneath them, who are really

00:16:43.059 --> 00:16:45.649
trying to get ahead, right, because that’s
where they’re hoping to get.

00:16:45.649 --> 00:16:51.009
It’s going to be the resentful and revengeful
few who think: well, the world’s fundamentally

00:16:51.009 --> 00:16:54.569
unfair and it’s obvious that those sons
of bitches got what they want from stealing

00:16:54.569 --> 00:16:59.089
it form me and here it turns out that if I
just go down the street and steal it back,

00:16:59.089 --> 00:17:05.750
well, not only am I allowed to do that but,
according to this new emergent ideology, man,

00:17:05.750 --> 00:17:08.760
that’s the best thing I could possibly do.

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:13.670
So then multiply that story by several million
participants and you have like the first five

00:17:13.670 --> 00:17:15.480
years of the Soviet empire.

00:17:15.480 --> 00:17:17.390
And so what do we have there?

00:17:17.390 --> 00:17:20.990
The result was a catastrophic onslaught on
millions of peasant households.

00:17:20.990 --> 00:17:28.000
At first, party activists and local officials,
read bullies, right, brutalized peasants,

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:30.510
forcing them to surrender their homesteads
and their possessions.

00:17:30.510 --> 00:17:34.820
Deportations, arrests and killings soon followed
as terror generalized.

00:17:34.820 --> 00:17:39.980
The violence mounted to full-scale rebellion
in various places, with regular troops engaged

00:17:39.980 --> 00:17:40.980
for months.

00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:45.539
For example, suppressing peasant uprisings.

00:17:45.539 --> 00:17:49.590
Resistance took various forms, usually reflecting
the hopeless, desperate anguish of a doomed

00:17:49.590 --> 00:17:50.590
population.

00:17:50.590 --> 00:17:54.850
In the Ukraine, there were even women’s
rebellions, spontaneous uprisings of peasant

00:17:54.850 --> 00:18:00.210
women who attacked the local Kolkhozes to
demand the return of confiscated farm products.

00:18:00.210 --> 00:18:06.830
With a colossal impact on the Soviet economy,
peasants slaughtered their animals by the

00:18:06.830 --> 00:18:09.760
millions rather than see them seized.

00:18:09.760 --> 00:18:12.450
For two years, the fighting raged.

00:18:12.450 --> 00:18:17.190
As the dreadful process of de-kulakization
continued, Stalin ordered a further assault

00:18:17.190 --> 00:18:19.210
on the recalcitrant peasantry.

00:18:19.210 --> 00:18:22.390
What Conquest calls the terror-famine of 1932.

00:18:22.390 --> 00:18:29.909
Moscow, writes Conquest, it’s from a book
called The Harvest of Sorrow, knowingly decreed

00:18:29.909 --> 00:18:34.889
grain procurements from the Ukraine and elsewhere
exceeding by far what the local population

00:18:34.889 --> 00:18:39.500
could produce, right, which meant that everyone
who lived there was forced and ordered to

00:18:39.500 --> 00:18:42.809
deliver more grain than they had ever grown.

00:18:42.809 --> 00:18:47.059
Communist brigades roamed the countryside
forcing agriculturalists to disgorge the little

00:18:47.059 --> 00:18:51.029
they had been able to produce under conditions
of severe dislocation.

00:18:51.029 --> 00:19:00.010
Grain sat unused in state reserves while the
local population starved.

00:19:00.010 --> 00:19:08.720
This is from Wisdom, apocryphal Biblical writings:
for the reasoned unsoundly saying to themselves:

00:19:08.720 --> 00:19:14.250
short and sorrowful is our life and there
is no remedy when a man comes to his end and

00:19:14.250 --> 00:19:17.160
no one has been known to return from Hades.

00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:21.769
Because we were born by mere chance and hereafter
we shall be as though we had never been because

00:19:21.769 --> 00:19:27.059
the breath in our nostrils is smoke and reason
a spark kindled by the beating of our hearts.

00:19:27.059 --> 00:19:30.539
When it is extinguished, the body will turn
to ashes and the spirit will dissolve like

00:19:30.539 --> 00:19:31.929
empty air.

00:19:31.929 --> 00:19:36.510
Our name will be forgotten in time and no
one will remember our works.

00:19:36.510 --> 00:19:40.409
Our life will pass away like the traces of
a cloud and be scattered like mist that is

00:19:40.409 --> 00:19:44.210
chased by the rays of the sun and overcome
by its heat.

00:19:44.210 --> 00:19:48.710
For our allotted time is the passing of a
shadow and there’s no return from our death

00:19:48.710 --> 00:19:51.610
because it is sealed up and no one turns back.

00:19:51.610 --> 00:19:57.370
So a piece of writing thousands of years old
and so Nietzsche says at the end of the 1900s:

00:19:57.370 --> 00:19:59.770
well, rationality undermines our faith in
religion.

00:19:59.770 --> 00:20:05.230
But we have a piece of writing from more than
2,000 years ago that says: look, what is it

00:20:05.230 --> 00:20:06.230
about being alive?

00:20:06.230 --> 00:20:09.059
It’s short and there’s nothing to it,
right?

00:20:09.059 --> 00:20:12.830
Our thoughts are biologically produced and
when we die there’s nothing left.

00:20:12.830 --> 00:20:17.500
Well, that’s a very modern thought yet it
was expressed thousands of years ago so you

00:20:17.500 --> 00:20:22.620
know, I think, merely from observing that
that the crisis of faith that characterizes

00:20:22.620 --> 00:20:28.010
modern society is a reflection of the permanent
crisis of faith that characterizes human beings.

00:20:28.010 --> 00:20:30.919
and you say: well, what’s happening with
the totalitarian?

00:20:30.919 --> 00:20:38.670
Well, the totalitarian is afraid of the unknown,
for good reason, I mean and he’s very interested

00:20:38.670 --> 00:20:42.039
in sustaining his own belief structure.

00:20:42.039 --> 00:20:47.559
And the combination of those two things, it
can start off trivially is that the more you’re

00:20:47.559 --> 00:20:52.149
convinced that you have to maintain the stability
of your current belief structure, the more

00:20:52.149 --> 00:20:56.260
afraid you are of anything that’s unknown.

00:20:56.260 --> 00:21:00.899
And the more afraid you are of anything that’s
unknown, the less likely you are to go out

00:21:00.899 --> 00:21:01.899
and explore.

00:21:01.899 --> 00:21:04.481
Then the less likely you are to go out and
explore, the weaker you get because you stop

00:21:04.481 --> 00:21:06.039
gather information.

00:21:06.039 --> 00:21:09.759
And then the weaker you get, the more necessary
it is that you have to have this frame of

00:21:09.759 --> 00:21:11.600
reference and it has to remain intact.

00:21:11.600 --> 00:21:15.049
And this sort of thing starts to cycle and
cycle.

00:21:15.049 --> 00:21:19.360
So you undermine your own sense of your own
autonomy and ability and you make yourself

00:21:19.360 --> 00:21:23.549
more and more a rigid tool of the propagandistic
system.

00:21:23.549 --> 00:21:28.460
And you more and more adopt a sense of enmity
towards anything you don’t understand.

00:21:28.460 --> 00:21:33.410
And that’s a spiral that goes rapidly down
hill, right, rapidly into a state that’s

00:21:33.410 --> 00:21:38.399
characterized by complete internal chaos
And I think that’s a good definition of

00:21:38.399 --> 00:21:42.149
what is meant in metaphysical language by
hell, right?

00:21:42.149 --> 00:21:43.470
Hell is a bottomless pit.

00:21:43.470 --> 00:21:44.470
Why?

00:21:44.470 --> 00:21:49.000
Well, I don’t care how bad things are for
you or around you, there’s always some bloody

00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:50.720
thing you could do to make it worse, right?

00:21:50.720 --> 00:21:54.620
There’s always some suffering you can extend
to others, there’s always some bit of stubbornness

00:21:54.620 --> 00:21:58.840
or rejection that you can pull off that will
make your already terrible situation worse,

00:21:58.840 --> 00:21:59.840
right?

00:21:59.840 --> 00:22:02.240
So there’s no bottom and that seems to me
to be right.

00:22:02.240 --> 00:22:07.700
If you do just a cursory historical analysis,
no matter what terrible account you can come

00:22:07.700 --> 00:22:12.720
across with regards to, say, concentration
camp brutality, in some other book there’s

00:22:12.720 --> 00:22:13.970
some worse story.

00:22:13.970 --> 00:22:20.000
Like limited only by the absolute ends of
the most brutal form of imagination, all a

00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:23.159
consequence, I think, of this process, right?

00:22:23.159 --> 00:22:28.289
And you can’t really say what causes it
because on the one hand, there’s cowardice

00:22:28.289 --> 00:22:30.210
and lack of faith, right?

00:22:30.210 --> 00:22:34.350
Anything I don’t understand – cowardice,
pride and lack of faith.

00:22:34.350 --> 00:22:38.610
Anything I don’t understand doesn’t exist,
plus I’m not the person to confront it anyways,

00:22:38.610 --> 00:22:39.610
right?

00:22:39.610 --> 00:22:40.690
That’s the lack of faith.

00:22:40.690 --> 00:22:45.559
Each of those things feeds into the other
and it’s very difficult to say where it

00:22:45.559 --> 00:22:46.559
starts.

00:22:46.559 --> 00:22:54.100
And the thing that’s kind of interesting
about these self-referential processes is

00:22:54.100 --> 00:22:58.730
that they don’t have to start dramatically.

00:22:58.730 --> 00:23:03.820
Like the loop can start very, very small and
it picks up speed very, very rapidly.

00:23:03.820 --> 00:23:08.140
So you imagine you’re speaking into a tape
recorder and the speaker’s on.

00:23:08.140 --> 00:23:12.070
You get too close to the speaker with the
microphone and you get some feedback.

00:23:12.070 --> 00:23:15.071
And if you bring the microphone a little closer,
the feedback develops more and more intensely.

00:23:15.071 --> 00:23:17.059
It can blow up the whole system.

00:23:17.059 --> 00:23:22.740
It doesn’t have to start dramatically to
move forward very, very rapidly.

00:23:22.740 --> 00:23:27.269
And what that means, at least in principle,
is that even small mistakes anywhere along

00:23:27.269 --> 00:23:32.419
this circle can start the development of precisely
this kind of spiral.

00:23:32.419 --> 00:23:36.490
And so you say: well, people, do people need
to be abused to become totalitarian?

00:23:36.490 --> 00:23:42.330
Well, and the answer to that is no because
everyone’s been abused sufficiently by some

00:23:42.330 --> 00:23:46.909
occurrences in their life to justify taking
a negative tack on the nature of experience.

00:23:46.909 --> 00:23:51.220
You say: well, how cowardly do you have to
be in order to run away from things?

00:23:51.220 --> 00:23:56.370
And you think: well, not that cowardly because
under most circumstances your life is characterized

00:23:56.370 --> 00:23:58.250
by sins of omission, right?

00:23:58.250 --> 00:24:04.040
It’s there are things you left undone
and like just exactly how rigid do you want

00:24:04.040 --> 00:24:05.400
your belief systems to be?

00:24:05.400 --> 00:24:10.100
And you say: well, I like them to be stable
because without that stability, then I’m

00:24:10.100 --> 00:24:11.100
terrified.

00:24:11.100 --> 00:24:15.379
And then you can say: well, fair enough but
that’s all a sign of a kind of existential

00:24:15.379 --> 00:24:16.379
weakness.

00:24:16.379 --> 00:24:20.250
And then if social circumstances come around
and give your life a good tweak, say like

00:24:20.250 --> 00:24:24.110
they did with the Germans prior to World War
II, you just never know what side you’re

00:24:24.110 --> 00:24:25.110
going to end up on.

00:24:25.110 --> 00:24:29.570
And so all these little tiny mistakes you
know, mistakes that I think are marked by

00:24:29.570 --> 00:24:33.720
your own conscience, are precisely that, leads
you down this terrible path.

00:24:33.720 --> 00:24:37.000
And if you think: well, no, that can’t be
right – well, then you have to remember

00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:42.879
that in these processes, say, of de-kulakization
and the immense wave of death that characterized

00:24:42.879 --> 00:24:46.840
the Soviet Union and Nazi Germany, most people
were involved.

00:24:46.840 --> 00:24:53.130
And if they weren’t involved in direct acts
of commission, they were absolutely involved

00:24:53.130 --> 00:24:55.210
in direct acts of omission, right?

00:24:55.210 --> 00:24:58.820
They knew but they didn’t say anything.

00:24:58.820 --> 00:25:03.970
Well, classically sins of commission are regarded
as much more evil, say, than sins of omission.

00:25:03.970 --> 00:25:05.919
But I actually think that’s backwards.

00:25:05.919 --> 00:25:11.419
The sins of omission are worse because every
time you walk away, I mean what do you do?

00:25:11.419 --> 00:25:13.929
You walk away from a Nazi, what are you walking
away from?

00:25:13.929 --> 00:25:16.470
Well, we know what you’re walking away from,
right?

00:25:16.470 --> 00:25:25.639
If you’re walking away from a domain that’s
likely to expand into something that’s completely

00:25:25.639 --> 00:25:31.290
undifferentiable from hell, and it’s no
wonder you walk away from that, but the fact

00:25:31.290 --> 00:25:36.259
that you walk away from it makes it much more
likely that it’s going to happen

00:25:36.259 --> 00:25:41.320
So then I think, to end this, something like
this.

00:25:41.320 --> 00:25:46.120
We look for economic reasons to explain great
terrible acts, right?

00:25:46.120 --> 00:25:51.059
We look for social reasons, we look for political
reasons but we have Nietzsche’s observation,

00:25:51.059 --> 00:25:55.009
which is something like this:
I don’t care whether or not your life’s

00:25:55.009 --> 00:25:57.379
been characterized by suffering and deprivation.

00:25:57.379 --> 00:26:02.179
The mere fact of suffering and deprivation
does not allow you to draw a particular conclusion.

00:26:02.179 --> 00:26:06.159
You can’t say that there’s a causal path
between economic deprivation, say, and the

00:26:06.159 --> 00:26:10.720
rise of a totalitarian state because any event
is susceptible to multiple interpretations.

00:26:10.720 --> 00:26:12.950
Well, how do these states come about?

00:26:12.950 --> 00:26:18.140
Well, I think: well, we look for political
and economic and social reasons because that’s

00:26:18.140 --> 00:26:20.070
the easiest place top look, right?

00:26:20.070 --> 00:26:24.990
If you ratchet up the level of description
to social forces that are beyond your control,

00:26:24.990 --> 00:26:28.020
then you never have to worry about what it
is that you’re doing or not doing that’s

00:26:28.020 --> 00:26:29.850
actually causing this sort of thing.

00:26:29.850 --> 00:26:33.909
But I think if you look at the historical
record, especially if you look at it from

00:26:33.909 --> 00:26:39.350
a mythological perspective, the story is basically
clear and it goes something like this:

00:26:39.350 --> 00:26:44.980
Every time you make a mistake that you know
is a mistake and you don’t fix it, the world

00:26:44.980 --> 00:26:46.860
moves more towards that.

00:26:46.860 --> 00:26:50.570
And it might be trivial, maybe, but it might
not be.

00:26:50.570 --> 00:26:53.919
So you look at Adolf Eichmann, for example,
and he’s a little bureaucrat who planned

00:26:53.919 --> 00:26:58.769
the Final Solution and you find out he’s
just a little ratty guy, right?

00:26:58.769 --> 00:27:02.500
You see him in a bar, you don’t even notice
him, he’s a negligible nobody.

00:27:02.500 --> 00:27:06.799
But he’s the guy who planned the Final Solution.

00:27:06.799 --> 00:27:11.659
He was a normal person, I mean maybe even
slightly less than normal, right?

00:27:11.659 --> 00:27:16.080
He is no monster, he wasn’t the sort of
person you’d remark on if you saw him.

00:27:16.080 --> 00:27:22.750
Precisely the opposite: invisible, quiet,
unassuming, presuming no doubt, at least until

00:27:22.750 --> 00:27:27.739
he was arrested, that he was just doing what
he was told and that was just fine.

